-- title:
--   Uniqueness of local maps from an unramified coefficient ring
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime and let $W_0$ be a commutative ring which is a domain and a discrete valuation ring, complete for the adic topology of its maximal ideal, whose maximal ideal is generated by the image of $q$, i.e. $\mathfrak{m}_{W_0} = (q)$, and whose residue field $\mathrm{ResidueField}\,W_0$ is perfect of characteristic exponent $q$ (the $q$-power Frobenius on it is bijective). Let $T$ be a commutative ring which is local and Artinian, and let $f, g : W_0 \to T$ be ring homomorphisms which are local (each carries non-units to non-units). Assume that $f$ and $g$ agree after composition with the residue map $T \to T/\mathfrak{m}_T$, that is, $\mathrm{residue}_T \circ f = \mathrm{residue}_T \circ g$ as ring homomorphisms $W_0 \to T/\mathfrak{m}_T$. The conclusion is that $f = g$.
--
--   This is the uniqueness half of the standard statement that an unramified complete discrete valuation ring with perfect residue field is a coefficient ring: a local homomorphism into an Artinian local ring is determined by the induced map on residue fields. It is used in the construction of the coefficient ring attached to a level moduli datum, in [`ModularCurve.LevelModuliPackageAbs.exists_coefficientRing_factorsThrough_adicCompletion_of_levelModuliDatum`](thm.html#ModularCurve.LevelModuliPackageAbs.exists_coefficientRing_factorsThrough_adicCompletion_of_levelModuliDatum).
